<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>Identifying Key Features of Graphed Functions</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m01997</md:content-id>
    <md:title>Identifying Key Features of Graphed Functions</md:title>
    <md:uuid>6ad2cf00-0ba3-4b8c-8110-e64da23933ca</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section class="numbered-exercises" id="sect-00001"><title>Cool Down</title>
    <para id="para-00001">The equation <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mo>-</m:mo><m:mn>16</m:mn><m:mi>t</m:mi><m:mo>-</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>t</m:mi><m:mo>-</m:mo><m:mn>1</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math> represents the height in feet of a beach ball thrown by a child as a function of time, <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>t</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>, in seconds.</para>
    
    <exercise id="_85f3be41-8f3f-4c2c-b8ea-ca5a6d375ec3" class="multiple-choice">
      <problem id="prob-00001">
  <para id="para-00004">Since the equation is in factored form, we can tell that the graph intersects the horizontal axis at two points. Use this information to find the zeros of the function. Explain or show your reasoning.</para>
<para id="para-00002">a. Find one of the zeros of the functions using <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mo>-</m:mo><m:mn>16</m:mn><m:mi>t</m:mi><m:mo>-</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math>.</para>

<para id="para-00005">b. Find one of the zeros of the functions using <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>t</m:mi><m:mo>-</m:mo><m:mn>1</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math>.</para>

        </problem>
        
        <solution id="sol-00001"><para id="para-00003">a. <m:math><m:mo>&#8722;</m:mo><m:mn>0.125</m:mn></m:math> or <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mo>-</m:mo><m:mfrac><m:mrow><m:mn>1</m:mn></m:mrow><m:mrow><m:mn>8</m:mn></m:mrow></m:mfrac></m:mrow></m:math>. From the factored form, we can tell that the graph intersects the horizontal axis at <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>t</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mo>–</m:mo><m:mn>0.125</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math> or <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mo>–</m:mo><m:mfrac><m:mrow><m:mn>1</m:mn></m:mrow><m:mrow><m:mn>8</m:mn></m:mrow></m:mfrac></m:mrow></m:math>. The zero of the function is the same value.</para>

       <para id="para-00006">b. 1. From the factored form, we can tell that the graph intersects the horizontal axis at t=1. The zero of the function is the same value.</para></solution></exercise><!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise id="exer-00001">
  <problem id="prob-00003">
    <para id="para-00008">What do the zeros tell us in this situation?</para>
    </problem><solution id="sol-00003">
    
    
  
  <para id="para-00009">Compare your answer: </para>
    
    <para id="para-00010">The zeros mean the time in seconds when the ball is on the ground.</para>
  
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->
<exercise id="_0c87020e-f0c2-47f7-b473-acf8830dad35" class="multiple-choice"><problem id="prob-00004"><para id="para-00011">Are both zeros meaningful?</para>
      </problem><solution id="sol-00004"><para id="para-00012">No. Only one of them is meaningful. The negative zero doesn't mean anything here.</para></solution></exercise>
<!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise id="exer-00002">
  <problem id="prob-00005">
    <para id="para-00014">From what height, in feet, is the beach ball thrown? Explain or show your reasoning.</para>
    </problem><solution id="sol-00005">
    
    
  
  <para id="para-00015">Compare your answer: </para>
    
    <para id="para-00016">2. The height when <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>t</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>0</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math> is <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mo>–</m:mo><m:mn>16</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>0</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>–</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>0</m:mn><m:mo>–</m:mo><m:mn>1</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mo>–</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mo>–</m:mo><m:mn>1</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math>. If you graph the function, the graph shows a <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>-intercepts of <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>0</m:mn><m:mo>,</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math>.</para>
  
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End --></section>
</content></document>